Type aliases & Interfaces: interface and type can describe the shape of an object.
Type aliases
The most common convention is to use PascalCase for type aliases:

type Product = {}
type User = {}
type Order = {}
type CartItem = {}
type PaymentDetails = {}

type Product = {
  id: number
  name: string
  price: number
}



interfaces
Why interface when their is type aliases? 
The main reason is that interface has some capabilities and behavior that type doesn't.
interface features such as extends or declaration merging.
interface Product {
  id: number
  name: string
  price: number
}

You can use extends interface:

interface Product {
  id: number
  name: string
}

interface DigitalProduct extends Product {
  downloadUrl: string
}

1. With type, you would typically use an intersection:

type Product = {
  id: number
  name: string
}

type DigitalProduct = Product & {
  downloadUrl: string
}

2. Interfaces support declaration merging: You can declare the same interface twice and TypeScript merges them into

interface Product {
  id: number
}

interface Product {
  name: string
}

TypeScript merges them into:


interface Product {
  id: number
  name: string
}

You cannot do the same with a type alias: The merging ability is useful, particularly useful in some libraries and frameworks where you want to extend existing types.

DATA BINDING
In Vue, data binding means connecting the data in your JavaScript/TypeScript code to the HTML template, so that when the data changes, Vue can automatically update the UI.
Two-way data binding using v-model



